Polyless wishless is _annoying_. (the other conducts are incidental)

Started this one with the idea of trying to play a fast game, but
totally failed at that; reflection sokoban, non-coaligned Minetown,
no altar, and no useful armor meant I finished Mines' End with AC 7 at
turn 12700. Why no armor? Why not wear mithril and throw daggers and get
to AC -2 or -5 or so with metal equipment? Well, my starting spell was
magic missile, so I decided that was better than daggers, or at least
that (magic missile plus daggers) was better than (daggers plus AC),
and I think that was true, at least for my playstyle.

At this point I decided to run for the wand, poking around for an altar
along the way, when I ran into a throne room with some rock trolls
or something that made my life miserable. After a close call there I
decided to use my scrolls of earth to wall in the Minetown priest and
convert the altar. (I killed the priest first, yay magic missile.)
This was a great choice. With a more reasonable AC 4 (gloves from a
watchman and the priest's robe) and Magicbane for MR and Elbereth, I
returned to my previous plan. 

The Quest level was the big room and it had a polytrap, but this worked in
my favor with infinite free Elbereth, and I soon had green dragon scales.
They were the only scales I saw all game. I immediately turned them into
armor and all of the sudden things looked different. Diving down further
found me a graveyard, which I wraith-lured to get to XL 14, and soon I had
the Eye and a host of fun new spells (is it just me or is the wizard quest
always _full_ of books?). Specifically, I had identify and magic mapping.
I began to seriously consider going wishless once I realized I had free 
action and levitation in my bag already.

When I found Ludios, I decided that I would, though I never did
anything other than convert the outer chamber and the vault it was
in into protection money. At my best I had AC -18, though I did all
of Gehennom with AC -10 or so and only 80-odd hit points. I levitated
around the Castle; the throne and wand had nothing to offer me and,
aside from Cleaver (and fire resistance from a red dragon --- I really
didn't have it yet! wow), the storerooms had nothing either.

The Valley got me a lot more tasty spells (I think that's where I
picked up Haste Self) and mapping my way up through Gehennom was largely
uneventful. I don't think I even bothered to sit on Vlad's throne; I used
skilled detect monster to keep an eye out for silver dragons, but no luck.
When I was done, I had only one scroll of gold detection, no conflict,
no genocide, and no magic markers. I began to regret saying "nothing" to
the djinni who came out of that blessed potion of enlightenment earlier...

Before waking the wizard I burned some time sacfesting to get crowned so
that I would have Finger of Death, and alchemized up to around 175 max HP.
I saved some blessed potions of full healing, which was _smart_, I think
I used four on the planes? I cleared out the Wizard's zoo on purpose
so I would have a corpse to use on him, and with the levport trick the
corpse got me all of the priests of Moloch, liches, and nalfshenees
hanging around in the Sanctum as well. Go down levitating and come up
jumping seems to be the way to go in that situation. Of course I had no
cursed potion of gain level but most graveyard monsters are magic missile
(the only spell I had time to time out on and relearn and care about)
fodder anyway.

Coming up was no problem, and I even found an extra scroll of
gold detection in each of the Wizard's tower and Sanctum, so I
was feeling pretty good.  Fire traps took my AC down to -14 but I
just hoped I would trip on a magic marker or even a scroll of enchant
armor...no. Grr. Eventually I hit DL1 and I wasn't about to start waiting,
so I went up the stairs with almost definitely my least prepared character
ever. (Even my illiterates had higher AC and HP, and both had access
to conflict.)

Earth I got through no problem thanks to a cockatrice corpse that I had
found on DL3 or so. Air was _harsh_ without conflict and without a valid
melee weapon to use on air elementals. I burned a bunch of death charges
and a lot of spell power on finger of death; the portal was also way over
in the upper right hand corner, possibly at the very corner. I made it
though, burning full healing but not lifesaving. (I kept reflection on
for lightning.)

Fire was no so bad, though the portal was in the very upper left corner,
so it burned through time and patience (which are totally finite resources
worth tracking, even though they don't show up on screen). It also used
my last scroll of gold detection; I was least afraid of water and so
I had chosen it to use a wand of secret door detection in. That turned
out to work OK though next time I would suck it up and do Earth the hard
way instead, there are no instadeath threats there.

Astral... ah, Astral. I was on the right side of the screen and so was
Death, so I went to the center with Pestilence (cure sickness at 0%
fail made that a good choice, I think). The Candelabrum was my light
source and jumping was my way of avoiding conflict but with only two
wands of teleportation (one of which I had to apply to survive) I was
really nervous about the possibility of a three-altar tour. No HoOA, of
course, because that would be _useful_. Getting to the first altar took
about half my resources, I'd say, and I did have two magic lamps, but...

Lucky for me, I got the right altar on the first try, so this is a victory
post :) This Astral was definitely outside of my comfort zone, though,
and it definitely would have been in my comfort zone had I had a ring
of conflict. Moral of the story: Conflict is powerful! And I could have
picked easier games to go wishless polyless on. But I'd never done them 
both at the same time before, so.

As a random side note, this was the first game I played entirely in color;
I blame Crawl for getting me in the habit of using color. (There were a 
couple of occasions where I said "oh no a ghost!!" at a rock piercer, but
that went away after a couple of hours.) Color is actually kind of good,
I may have been silly not to use it before.

